INFO 7705 :: Page 47 :: 9 P. M Ft Monroe Va Jany 17th 1865 Ft Monroe 630 PM 17th for Honorable G. V. Fox Asst. Secy <deletion>Navy</deletion> Navy Washn. ---- After three days& nights bombardment Fort Fisher is ours with all contiguous works commanding New Inlet ---- Assault was made by Army & Naval Brigade three oclock Sunday afternoon. One corner of Fort was secured in half hour but hand to hand fight with garrison lasted until 9 - oclock at night. Stubborn & bloody resistance. Fort & approaches strewn with dead ---- garrison had been heavily reinforced & number of prisoners taken over two thousand. Guns captured seventy two. All the Forts including Mound & Lukes Island Battery surrendered Rebel loss in assault five hundred dead besides the wounded Our loss Army & Navy about nine hundred fleet Lieut Preston & Lieut Porter Comdr of flag ship both killed in assault Genl Whiting & Lamb both prisoners & wounded. Rebel pirates Tallahasse & Chick = a = maugo both in fight & driven up river our gunboats went up river on Monday morning. Our prisoners will be imm'y sent North ---- we had seven days of delightfully calm weather ---- magazine in Fort exploded by accident monday morning killing two hundred our men Santiago brings bodies Porter & Preston & Naval wounded, goes to Norfolk for repairs ---- Please send dispatch to "American" tonight sig Chas C. Fulton
INFO 7706 :: Page 48 :: 9. P. M. via Cairo 17th 1865 New Orleans La Jany 12th New Orleans Jany. 12th Gen Halleck. Sir. I have the honor to request that Gen Gilmore U S. Vols may be ordered to report to me for duty in this Dept. If not in = consistent with the interest of the services I beg that he may be relieved from the board of which he is a member & directed to return to this city as soon as practicable very Respectfully Your Obt Servt Ed R. S. Canby Maj Gen. Comdg 1050 AM City Pt Jany 18th 1865 9 30 AM. 18th for Secy War Ft Monroe I was just starting to Ft Monroe to meet you when your dispatch was rec'd announcing that you were about starting for Washn Your dispatch of 10. P. M the 17th did not reach me until about one this morning and was imm'y replied to U. S. Grant Lt Genl
INFO 7707 :: Page 49 :: 1 P. M. 18th Paducah Ky. Jan. 18 1865. Paducah 18th C. A. Dana Asst. Secy War. Washn ---- Your two dispatches of yesterday are rec'd ---- In accordance with my last dispatch we left yesterday morning. Last night we met the Entire 2d Divn & two brigades of the 3rd Gen McLean Comdg 9 thousand strong & much overloaded Ascertaining that I had provided <insertion>transportation</insertion> ample for the remainder & deeming it more important that I should return here I detailed a very competent <insertion>officer</insertion> Capt. Arthur Edwards, to proceed & supervise the embarking at Clifton ---- Gen McLeans command with additional boats left this morning at Eight oclk & should reach Louisville on friday morning. After perfecting arrangements here I shall take rails tonight to Cairo & be at Louisville tomorrow. With the present very high river transports come from Eastport to Paducah in 16 to 20 hours from Clifton in 12 to 16 hours, from Paducah to Louisville two days Cincinnati 3 days Parkers - burg or Wheeling five to 6 days The remainder of the Corps from Clifton should be here between this & Friday morning Transpn is required for 18 to 20 thousand with five Batteries of mounted Artillery Dispatches will reach me at Cairo until mid night Please advise the Balto & Ohio RR Signed L. B. Parsons Colonel & Chf Rail & River Transpn. <deletion>morning</deletion>
INFO 7708 :: Page 50 :: 7 P. M. 8 PM City Point Va Jan. 18. 1865 Secy of War ---- The confirmation of Thomas & Sheridan to the rank of Maj. Genls in the regular army makes two vacancies, I hope Gen Terry will get one of these US Grant Lt Gen Recd 10 PM 18th Eastport Jan. 1 1865. HdQrs Eastport Miss. Jan 16th 9 P. M. for Gen Halleck ---- Your telegram of 1030 AM 12th also the Hon Asst Sec War telegram of 2 PM 11th were recd today ---- Schofield commenced <insertion>embarking</insertion> his troops yesterday the 15th ---- he consulted with me as to the propriety of taking his troops on boats as far up the Ohio as possible which I authorized him to do ---- I will forward him a copy of your instructions by dispatch boats & I hope he will be able to communicate with Col Parsons by telegraph from Paducah and perfect the arrangements for a comfortable & speedy trip Geo. H. Thomas M. G. C
INFO 7709 :: Page 51 :: Recd City Point Va Jan 18. 1865 11 PM 18th 9 PM Gen Halleck I I understand that Beauregard has gone west to gather up what can be saved from Hood's Army to bring against Sherman; If this be the case Selma & Montgomery will be easily reached ---- I do not think believe though that Thomas will ever get there from the North, he is too ponderous in his preparations & equipment to move through a country rapidly enough to live off of it ---- west of the Mississippi we do not want to do more than defend what we now hold but I do want Gen Canby to make a winter campaign either from Mobile bay or from some point in Florida you might order all the Cavalry horses now in the west to Gen Canby & direct him to make an independent campaign looking to the capture of Mobile, first, if the job does not promise too promise too long a one, & Montgomery & Selma ---- & the destruct - tion of all roads machine shops & stores the main object ---- Thomas can do with out horses for some time; a portion of his troops could be sent by water to Gen Canby; if Thomas does move in cooperation probably the best route for him to take would be by way of Chattanooga, Repairing the road to Rome & starting from there ---- These I give as views ---- what I would order is that Gen Canby be furnished cavalry horses & be directed to prepare to commence a campaign & that Thomas be teleghed to see what he could do; & when ; & get his views upon the choice of routes looking upon Selma as his objective point ---- ---- Thomas must make a campaign or spare his surplus troops sig U. S. Grant Lt Gen = 930 PM Gen Halleck ---- It will not not do for Canby to rely on either Granger or Hurlbut as first in command of an important campaign Sig U. S. Grant Lt Genl
