INFO 8522 :: Page 44 :: 430 PM 16th Eastport Miss Jany 15th 1865 Hd Qrs Eastport Miss Jany 15th 12 M for Gen Halleck . I would respectfully ask permission from the Dept to send to Washington the officers & enlisted men belonging to my army who have captured battle flags and other trophies from the enemy in the recent battle in Tennessee . I desire to send them with the captured trophies to be delivered at the War offices. Geo H. Thomas Maj Gen Comd'g 430 P. M 16th Eastport Jany 15th 1865 Hd Qrs Dpt Cumb Eastport Miss Jany 15th 12 M for Gen Halleck . The fleet is ready for the troops of Schofield Corps to embark which he is now doing . He will proceed to his destin ation as rapidly as possible - bord Geo H Thomas 330 PM 5 PM City Point Jan. 16. 1865 Gen Halleck ---- Annapolis was named as the destination of Schofield's corps for the reason that it was most favorable for embarkation , & it was supposed by the time it reached there present operations of the armies would develop the point most important to send it . If you deem it best however , you may change its destination to Alexandria US Grant Lt Gen
INFO 7700 :: Page 42 :: 10 P. M. Maj Eckert Ft. Monroe Va Jan 15. 1865 Following for your information, HdQrs Expeditionary force before Wilmington N. C. Jan 13th via Ft. Monroe 7 pm 15th for Br Gen John A. Rawlins Chf. Stf. City Pt. ---- Genl, After consultation with Col Comstock & in view of what I think is the ascertained fact that the whole or nearly the whole of Hoke's force is still here I think that it would be Genl Grant send the balance of the troops which Genl Wane spoke advisable to send the balance of the troops which <insertion>Genl Grant</insertion> spoke of sending forward ---- It seems indispensable to hold strongly the line towards Wilmington & for this purpose at least 4 of my brigades are necessary leaving only 2 brigades for operations against the fort ---- in case an assault should be tried & be unsuccessful the losses which would be incurred would leave an entirely insufficient force for further operations ---- I suggest that those which come should be sent in vessels cape - able of standing a storm at their anchors here & should be provisioned coaled & watered for at least 15 days so that in case the weather should prevent the landing of supplies for more troops than we now have they could remain on the vessels ---- I have the honor to be Genl, very respy. your obt Servt - Alfred H. Terry Brevet Maj Genl Comdg Expedition P. S. Please send me twenty panlines to cover stores & ammunition A. H. Terry. B. M. G. J.
INFO 7701 :: Page 43 :: 530 PM City Pt Jany 16th 1865 Gen Halleck The following has been rec'd from Gen Terry ---- ---- Orders have been given for Everything required 10 PM US Grant Lt Gen Hd Qrs Expeditionary force before Wilmington N. C. Jan 13th via Ft Monroe 7 PM 15th for Br Gen Jno A Rawlins Chf Stf City Pt. ---- Genl, we arrived here last night & disembarked today getting the Infantry on shore at 3PM ---- our ammunition is landed & most of the stores ---- we hold a line across the point from the flag Pond battery but it is not a good one & probably will have to be changed during this night ---- as soon as the line is determined upon I shall force it ---- Today I intend to move down troops under cover of the Navy fire & determine whether an assault is advisable ---- Prisoners & deserters report that Hokes Div is here or that 3 brigades are here ---- A portion of the ammunition <insertion>in the</insertion> men's boxes was spoiled getting through the surf ---- Please send me 3 hundred thousand rounds of calibre 58 ---- I think that 50 wagons & teams should also be sent down, they will be useful in any event ---- I have the honor to be Gen. very respy Your obt. servt ---- Alfred H Terry Bvt. Maj Gen Comdg Expedition (See opposite (page ----)
INFO 7702 :: Page 44 :: 12.10 P. M. Beckwith City Pt Ft. Monroe Va Jan. 16. 1865. 10.30AM for Col T. S. Bowers A. A. G. City Pt. ---- Steamer California leaves here 12 oclock ---- Capt of transport returned from Expedition to Wilmington reports all forces safely & nicely landed on friday last & skirmishing going on about fort Fisher ---- they suppose it already in our possession ---- of course none of troops that left here last are included ---- very respy Yr Obt. Servt Signed H. V. Robinett Capt. & A. D. C. 710 PM Ft Monroe Va Jany 16th 1865 630 P. M. Transport "Varana" just in left off fort Fisher yesterday morning 10 oclock Captain says he saw six siege guns landed with trains. Our troops were all landed & had entrenched two lines across peninsula ---- had captured one of the Enemy's boats on the river & burnt it. weather splendid outside yesterday & today ---- no attack had been made on our troops and the force of the enemy unknown. ---- Geo D. Sheldon
INFO 7703 :: Page 45 :: 6.15 P. M Cairo Ill Jany 16th 1865 Jany. 16th 430 P. M. for Hon C. A Dana Arrived here this morning. start for Paducah immediately. ---- Will have boats enough there in the morning for the movement and start early unless detained for convoy. Hope to be at Clifton early Wednesday & start back on Thursday weather & river favorable A cipher telegraph operation accompanied me according to your order ---- L. B. Parsons Col & Chf Q. M 1 PM Ft Monroe Va Jan 17. 1865. 1230 P. M for CA Dana ---- The Tribune Special Corres. with the Wilmington Expedn sends the following to Hon Asst Secy Dana ---- fort Fisher is ours & with it all the adjacent defences of New Inlet ---- It surrendered to our troops under Gen. Terry after a well sustained assault of 4 hours, at 9 30 P. M. Sunday 15th ---- Nothing could withstand the bravery of our troops notwithstanding the garrison was composed of two thousand picked South & North Carolina troops ----
